WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
Solana’s original design tracks order. Can swapping “add two” and “double” change our answer?

2
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:20.544
Yes. Starting from one, adding first gives six. Doubling first gives four. The order changes the answer.

3
00:00:20.544 --> 00:00:30.544
A calculation makes a fingerprint. In Proof of History, does each new fingerprint use the one before it?

4
00:00:31.044 --> 00:00:41.088
Yes, each result feeds the next calculation. Each new result waits for the previous one, building a linked trail.

5
00:00:41.088 --> 00:00:51.088
A message joins the trail here. Can computers check that it was included before the next step?

6
00:00:51.588 --> 00:01:01.632
Yes, the message helps make the next fingerprint. These links let computers check where messages joined the trail.

7
00:01:01.632 --> 00:01:11.632
With each piece’s start and end saved, can computers check different pieces at the same time?

8
00:01:12.132 --> 00:01:22.176
Yes. Saved fingerprints let computers check separate pieces together. Building needs linked steps. Checking can happen side-by-side.

9
00:01:22.176 --> 00:01:32.176
A trail’s calculations check out. Does that mean the network has already agreed to accept that record?

10
00:01:32.676 --> 00:01:42.720
No. The network still needs its voting rules to choose a shared record. Checking calculations and reaching agreement have different jobs.

11
00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:52.720
If one checking step is quick, must the message’s whole journey be quick too?

12
00:01:53.220 --> 00:02:03.120
No. Messages can still wait for delivery or a busy computer. The whole journey depends on all its parts.
